power: UMP9620 boards: one check for the board with a battery

The F50 and the U30 Air share the UMP9620 device tree layout, charger, fuel gauge and USB-PD nodes included; only
the U30 Air has a battery, and only its tree has the stock charger policy service. The bq256xx charger (0016),
the fuel gauge's awake events (0023) and the port controller (0020) each looked the node up on their own and took
its mere presence for the answer. One helper, ump9620_has_battery_policy() in <linux/power/ump9620-battery.h>,
answers for all three, and asks for the node to be enabled as well. The idea is kanoqwq's (clean-tf-7.2,
"battery board isolation").

--- a/drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c
@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@
 #include <linux/extcon.h>
 #include <linux/mfd/sc27xx-pmic.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/power/ump9620-battery.h>
 #include <linux/spi/spi.h>
 
 #define BQ256XX_MANUFACTURER "Texas Instruments"
@@ -2286,7 +2287,6 @@
 	if (bq->bq2560x_chg) {
 		struct bq256xx_chip_info *info;
 		int part, err;
-		struct device_node *np;
 		bool battery;
 
 		/* the bq25601 as it is, without a register cache and with its input detection */
@@ -2311,9 +2311,7 @@
 		 * told to charge, the fuel gauge reported over-voltage and the PMIC reset the board, over and over
 		 * on a router's USB port (issue #225). There it stays unbound, as before v2026.10.25.
 		 */
-		np = of_find_node_by_path("/charger_policy_service");
-		battery = np;
-		of_node_put(np);
+		battery = ump9620_has_battery_policy();
 		if (part < 0 && client->addr != SGM41513_I2C_ADDR && battery) {
 			err = part;
 			chip = devm_i2c_new_dummy_device(dev, client->adapter, SGM41513_I2C_ADDR);
